ique input gets computed once even across multiple calls, and repeated inputs reuse that result. If any single input fails, the whole batch returns nil with the error.\n\nNow I need to verify how missing dependency references get caught—whether that validation happens during parse.Load or later in the graph ordering step.\n\nTracing through Kahn's algorithm with a missing dependency like \"missing\" that's not a defined node: the indegree for \"x\" would be 1 since it depends on \"missing\", so \"x\" never enters the queue, and the algorithm would wrongly report a cycle rather than the real issue of an undefined dependency. That confirms missing deps need their own explicit check in parse.Load rather than relying on graph.Order to catch them.\n\nSince the test only checks that an error is returned, any error would technically pass, but I want accurate messaging, so I'll have parse.Load validate for empty names, duplicate names, and missing dependencies, while graph.Order handles genuine cycle detection via Kahn's algorithm. Now I'm starting to write the graph package with the Node and Graph type definitions...\n\nWriting Kahn's algorithm...\n\nI'm checking against the graph length to detect cycles, returning an error if not all nodes were processed. Since missing dependencies are already validated during parsing, any unresolved indegree at this stage would only indicate an actual cycle.\n\nTracing through the test cases: for two independent nodes \"a\" and \"b\", both start with zero indegree, get queued in sorted order, and pop out as [\"a\", \"b\"], matching the expected result with no cycle detected. For the self-cycle case where node \"x\" depends on itself, indegree starts at 1, the queue stays empty, and the loop never runs -- leaving result shorter than the graph, correctly flagging a cycle.\n\nNow I'm writing the parse package, which unmarshals JSON into a list of nodes, builds the graph map while checking for empty names and duplicates, then does a second pass validating that every dependency referenced actually exists in the graph.\n\nNext I'm implementing a cache wrapper around plan.Build that keys off the raw input bytes, returning cached results when available and storing new ones otherwise. Now I'm starting on the batch package that will use this cache...\n\nWriting batch loop...\n\nThen I'm writing a CLI entry point that reads stdin, passes it to plan.Build, and prints the result or errors out; next I want to check the go.mod file for the module path.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1455,"cache_read_input_tokens":17164,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1455,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"27c0f7d8-cbef-4a29-808e-dbad78343b11","uuid":"d4da24de-0e85-4b1a-986e-7b2a56f1ea52"}
